WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 33: The Vigilantes of Alder Gulch and the 3-7-77 Secret Code

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:16.240
[Rebecca] In the summer of 1863, in the midst of the Civil War, gold was discovered along a remote creek in southwestern Montana known as Alder Gulch.

4
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:24.720
[Rebecca] Within months, over 10,000 prospectors poured into Virginia City, pulling millions of dollars in raw gold dust from the frozen gravel.

5
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.320
[Rebecca] But getting the gold out of the mountains was a deadly gamble.

6
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:37.280
[Rebecca] Along the isolated stagecoach trails, heavily armed highwaymen known as road agents ambushed travelers, murdering more than 100 men in cold blood.

7
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:46.880
[Rebecca] The terror seemed unstoppable until the miners discovered that the mastermind of the murderous gang was none other than the charming, newly elected Sheriff of Banach, Henry Plummer.

8
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:54.800
[Rebecca] What followed was a swift, merciless campaign of frontier justice that gave birth to the mysterious symbol 3777.

9
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.520
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

10
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.440
[George] In their very first pan, they found $2.40 in coarse gold dust.

11
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:21.280
[George] It was the discovery of Alder Gulch, the richest placer gold strike in human history.

12
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:32.000
[George] Within months, a chaotic string of boomtown camps, anchored by Virginia City, sprang up along 14 miles of the gulch, swelling to more than 10,000 residents.

13
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:45.520
[George] Millions of dollars in raw gold bullion were extracted from the stream beds every month, flowing through roaring saloons, hurtigurdy dance halls, and gambling dens where miners slept with loaded six guns beneath their blankets.

14
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:52.640
[George] But the mining camps were completely isolated, located hundreds of miles from the nearest federal territorial court.

15
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.200
[George] Law and order simply did not exist.

16
00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:06.040
[George] The mountainous stage trails between Virginia City, Banach, and Salt Lake City were terrorized by an organized syndicate of ruthless highwayman who called themselves the innocents.

17
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:23.240
[George] Recognizing one another by a secret password and a distinct sailor's knot tied in their neckerchiefs, the road agents ambushed freight wagons, stagecoaches, and lone prospectors, murdering their victims in cold blood, so that no witnesses could ever testify against them.

18
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:33.240
[George] In less than a single year, over 100 travelers vanished into the mountain passes, their bullet-tridled corpses stripped of gold and abandoned in remote gulches.

19
00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:45.960
[George] Prospectors slept with their heavy buckskin pokes of gold dust buried beneath the dirt floors of their tents, terrified by rumors that masked riders were watching every stagecoach departure from the surrounding ridgelines.

20
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:54.440
[George] Desperate for protection, the miners of Banach and Virginia City held public elections in the summer of 1863 to appoint a sheriff.

21
00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.520
[George] Their choice was Henry Plummer.

22
00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:07.080
[George] Plummer was a handsome, polished 27-year-old gentleman from Maine who possessed impeccable manners, dressed in fine tailored suits, and spoke with refined eloquence.

23
00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:11.000
[George] He was also a deadly, lightningfast shot with a revolver.

24
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:17.240
[George] The miners viewed Plummer as their courageous savior, electing him sheriff with overwhelming support.

25
00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:22.040
[George] What the honest miners did not know was that Plummer lived a terrifying double life.

26
00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:26.360
[George] He was, in fact, the supreme commander and mastermind of the innocents.

27
00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:34.760
[George] Plummer had a violent criminal past in California and Nevada, having previously escaped from San Quentin prison after killing a man.

28
00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:39.480
[George] As sheriff, Plummer organized the road agents with corporate efficiency.

29
00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:46.400
[George] He positioned his gang members as deputy sheriffs, blacksmiths, and saloon bartenders along the main trail.

30
00:03:46.640 --> 00:04:00.800
[George] Whenever a prospector prepared to leave town with a heavy buckskin pouch of gold dust, Plummer's spies marked the traveler's stagecoach with a chalk mark on the chassis, signaling road agents waiting in mountain ambushes twenty miles away.

31
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:07.440
[George] For months, Plummer maintained his facade of civic virtue while pocketing a cut of every murder on the trail.

32
00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:19.760
[George] Plummer maintained a charming, polite demeanor at church socials and town dances, smiling warmly at the very merchants and miners whose stagecoaches his men would plunder the following morning on the frozen trails.

33
00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:25.680
[George] The breaking point arrived in December 1863 with the brutal murder of Nicholas Tybalt.

34
00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:33.440
[George] Tybalt was a gentle, hardworking young German immigrant who had been hired to deliver a team of mules to a buyer in Virginia City.

35
00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:42.160
[George] When Tybalt failed to return, a local rancher hunting lost cattle nine miles outside of town stumbled upon a horrifying discovery.

36
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.080
[George] Tybalt's frozen corpse was concealed in a dense willow thicket.

37
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:58.880
[George] He had been shot through the head, his hands were bound with rawhide rope, and a lariat was cinched tightly around his neck, proving that he had been dragged through the snow while still alive before being robbed of two hundred dollars.

38
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:06.880
[George] When Tybalt's frozen body was brought into the center of Virginia City in an open wagon, the miners erupted in volcanic rage.

39
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:15.680
[George] The miners had tolerated robberies, saloons and gambling brawls, but the sadistic torture of an innocent, unarmed youth was intolerable.

40
00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:24.160
[George] Hundreds of armed miners surrounded the town, resolved that if the civil courts would not protect them, they would execute justice themselves.

41
00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] The sight of young Tybalt lying frozen in the wagon bed crystallized the fury of the mining community.

42
00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:37.800
[George] Honest prospectors realized that without decisive collective action, Alder Gulch would become an open slaughterhouse.

43
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:51.720
[George] On December 23, 1863, five prominent citizens, including rancher Paris Fouts and lawyer Wilbur Fisk Sanders, gathered secretly by candlelight in the back of an adobe cabin in Virginia City.

44
00:05:51.720 --> 00:06:03.880
[George] Raising their right hands, they swore a solemn, binding oath to form the Montana Vigilance Committee, a disciplined, underground military tribunal dedicated to exterminating the road agents.

45
00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:12.360
[George] Within days, hundreds of honest miners took the secret oath, organizing into paramilitary squads commanded by captains and lieutenants.

46
00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:16.440
[George] The vigilantes moved with lethal speed and absolute secrecy.

47
00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:23.240
[George] They arrested a low-level outlaw named George Ives, who had been seen in possession of Tybalt's stolen mules.

48
00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:29.880
[George] After an outdoor trial held before a jury of 24 miners in the snow, Ives was convicted of murder.

49
00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:40.040
[George] On the evening of December 21, under the watchful rifles of 300 armed miners, Ives was stood on a wooden packing crate and hanged from a timber beam.

50
00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:48.040
[George] Before he died, Ives whispered names, confirming that Sheriff Henry Plummer was the supreme chieftain of the road agent syndicate.

51
00:06:48.040 --> 00:07:01.000
[George] The vigilantes conducted their proceedings with solemn, military gravity, refusing to tolerate drunken lynch mobs and insisting upon sworn testimony and proper verification before a noose was ever dropped.

52
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:07.400
[George] On Sunday, January 10, 1864, the vigilantes struck at the head of the serpent.

53
00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:15.520
[George] A heavily armed squad of fifty vigilantes rode thirty miles through a howling blizzard into Bannock, surrounding Sheriff Henry Plummer's home.

54
00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:24.800
[George] Taken completely by surprise, Plummer was disarmed without a fight, along with his two murderous deputies, Ned Ray and Buck Stinson.

55
00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:34.160
[George] The vigilantes marched the three prisoners down the snowy street, toward the frozen gallows that Plummer himself had constructed months earlier to hang a horse thief.

56
00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:41.680
[George] Realizing his charm and eloquence could not save him, Plummer broke down in tears, begging on his knees for his life.

57
00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:49.360
[George] Save my life, boys, cut off my ears, strip me naked and turn me out in the freezing snow, but do not hang me.

58
00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:51.760
[George] I have an innocent wife in Nevada.

59
00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:58.080
[George] The grim vigilantes were unmoved, remembering the dozens of innocent travelers murdered in cold blood.

60
00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:01.840
[George] Plummer's deputies were hanged first, cursing loudly.

61
00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:09.760
[George] Plummer then composed himself, removed his fine silk necktie, and said quietly, Give me a good drop, boys.

62
00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:15.840
[George] The rope was tossed over the center beam, and the sheriff swung into eternity in the freezing mountain wind.

63
00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:22.720
[George] Plummer had designed the Banach gallows with elaborate carpentry, boasting that it would rid the territory of thieves.

64
00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:29.760
[George] In the supreme irony of frontier history, the handsome sheriff became the first condemned man to hang from its beams.

65
00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:39.360
[George] Over the next six weeks, the vigilante posses rode relentlessly across Montana's frozen river basins, tracking down every member of Plummer's syndicate.

66
00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:49.680
[George] Twenty-four road agents were arrested, tried, and hanged from gallows, corral crossbeams, and ceiling rafters, effectively annihilating the criminal gang.

67
00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:59.640
[George] Whenever an outlaw or criminal sympathizer was targeted for expulsion from the territory, they would wake in the morning to find a terrifying message pinned to their cabin door.

68
00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.480
[George] The numbers 3777 chalked in bold strokes.

69
00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:09.560
[George] The exact meaning of 3777 remains one of the West's greatest mysteries.

70
00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:17.720
[George] Some historians believe it represented the dimensions of a grave, three feet wide, seven feet long, and seventy-seven inches deep.

71
00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:24.360
[George] Others believe it stood for three hours, seven minutes and seventy-seven seconds given to leave town.

72
00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:28.600
[George] Still others suggest it referenced a secret Masonic voting symbol.

73
00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:35.160
[George] Regardless of its origin, the symbol struck absolute terror into the hearts of outlaws across the territory.

74
00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:47.800
[George] The mysterious numbers appeared on corral gates, hotel mirrors, and tent canvas across Virginia City, sending cold chills down the spines of hardened outlaws who abandoned their horses and fled on foot.

75
00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:59.240
[George] Today, more than a century and a half after the hangings at Banach and Virginia City, the legacy of the Alder Gulch vigilantes remains an indelible part of Montana's cultural identity.

76
00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:13.000
[George] In 1935, when the Montana State Legislature established the Montana Highway Patrol to police the vast highways of the Treasure State, the patrol's first commander chose a striking, unprecedented emblem for the force.

77
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:23.160
[George] Emblazoned upon the shoulder patch of every uniformed state trooper, and painted prominently on the doors of every patrol cruiser, are the bold golden numbers.

78
00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.160
[George] 3777.

79
00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:39.800
[George] It is the only law enforcement agency in the United States to wear a historic vigilante emblem on its official uniform, while modern historians debate the legality and morality of extrajudicial frontier tribunals, to the people of Montana.

80
00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:53.760
[George] The symbol represents the timeless resolve of honest citizens who banded together when civilization was absent to protect their families, establish order in the wilderness, and ensure that the rule of law would take root in the mountains.

81
00:10:54.080 --> 00:11:02.240
[Rebecca] The vigilantes of Alder Gulch represent the raw, uncompromising struggle for justice in a frontier wilderness devoid of courts or law.

82
00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:08.800
[Rebecca] It reminds us that when institutions fail, the human desire for safety and order will always forge a path forward.

83
00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:11.680
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

84
00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:12.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

85
00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:17.680
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we head to Wyoming for the Cattle War of Johnson County.

86
00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:27.200
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

